window.docContents[239].resolve({"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/The-Three-Dimensional-Regularity-Problem/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--The-Three-Dimensional-Regularity-Problem--The-Navier___Stokes-Millennium-Problem--Verified-computation-and-the-open-problem":{"contents":"For the exact rotation and forced shear flows, the symbolic proofs establish\nincompressibility and vanishing of each momentum residual component in Lean.\nNeither velocity has finite energy,\nso neither is admissible data for the Clay alternatives. On rational spacetime\nboxes, CEGA can certify scalar bounds for supported residual expressions after\ntheir derivative formulas have been justified. A bound in a chosen vector or\nfunction norm needs an explicit reduction to these certificates; with that\nreduction, estimates take the form\n\n\n\nA computer-assisted proof for a nontrivial Navier–Stokes regime would combine\nsuch bounds with an analytic validation theorem converting residual bounds into\na nearby true solution, quantitative control of a continuation norm, and an\nargument covering the entire time interval. The exact CAS checks, the interval\ncertificates, and the abstract continuation alternative are formalized; such a\nvalidation theorem is not.\n\nThe simplest solved datum checks that the formal Clay statement connects\ncorrectly to the core equation.\n\nFor every positive viscosity, the zero initial velocity with zero forcing has\na global smooth finite-energy solution:Its kinetic energy vanishes for every nonnegative time, so the existence\npredicate of alternative (A) holds for this datum.\n\nThe global zero classical solution supplies smooth velocity and pressure on\nthe physical half-space. The initial velocity is divergence free, and it and\nthe force are smooth and rapidly decreasing because they vanish identically.\nEvery term in the momentum equation is zero, and the kinetic energy is zero at\nevery time.\nAny positive constant therefore gives the strict uniform energy bound required\nby alternative (A) for this datum.\n\nAlternative (A) asks for the same conclusion for every admissible initial\nvelocity; alternative (C) asks, at each viscosity, for one admissible datum and\nforce where it fails.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009The Three-Dimensional Regularity Problem\u0009The Navier–Stokes Millennium Problem","header":"8.7.1.5. Verified computation and the open problem","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/The-Three-Dimensional-Regularity-Problem/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--The-Three-Dimensional-Regularity-Problem--The-Navier___Stokes-Millennium-Problem--Verified-computation-and-the-open-problem"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Weak-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Aubin___Lions-Compactness--Compactness-from-space-and-time-regularity":{"contents":"Spatial compactness alone leaves an obvious escape route: oscillation in\ntime. Fix  and put\n on  for positive integers . These\ntrajectories are uniformly bounded in , yet for ,\n\n\n\nThey have no strongly convergent subsequence in that space. Their time\nderivatives grow like  in , which is exactly the\nbehavior the second Aubin–Lions bound excludes.\n\nThe Aubin–Lions argument has a spatial half and a temporal half. In the\ntemporal half, Lions–Magenes regularity of the time derivative gives\nevery finite-dimensional observable of a bounded family a common Hölder\nmodulus of order one half, and Arzelà–Ascoli then makes those observables\nprecompact.\n\nLet  be a Gelfand triple with inclusion , let\n, and let  be a family with  and\n, each equipped with a Lions–Magenes continuous pivot\nrepresentative  whose integrated dual derivative is\n. For a fixed test vector  and all ,If  for all  and , and\n for all ,\nthen the scalar coefficient curves , regarded\nas bounded continuous functions on , have compact closure in the\nuniform norm.For a finite-dimensional trial space  with a stable trial\nprojection , the projected curves satisfy the analogous moduluswhere  is the pivot-mass-inverted restriction of the dual\nderivative to , and under uniform bounds  and  on the two\nquantities the family  has compact closure in\n. The underlying abstract step is that a uniformly bounded family\nof continuous curves into a proper normed space with a common modulus\n is relatively compact in the\nuniform norm.\n\nThe integrated dual derivative gives the pairing identity\n, and\nCauchy–Schwarz in time bounds the interval integral of an  function\nby  times its  norm. For the projection, the increment\n is the Bochner integral of the mass-inverted\nrestricted dual derivative, and the same estimate applies. The pivot bound\nand Cauchy–Schwarz bound the curves uniformly, so they take values in a\ncompact ball of the finite-dimensional target; the common modulus gives\nequicontinuity, and Arzelà–Ascoli yields compact closure.\n\nNow combine the two halves. Compactness of  supplies\nfinite-dimensional projections  with\n\n\n\nAfter integrating in time, the discarded spatial tail is uniformly small\nfor the whole bounded family. Fix  first: the time-derivative bound\ncontrols the finitely many retained coefficient curves, so temporal\ncompactness makes those curves precompact. Choose a finite approximation for\nthem and then add back the small tail. This order matters: the temporal\nconstants may depend on , while the final conclusion is strong\ncompactness in ; strong compactness in  does not follow.\n\nLet  be real Hilbert spaces with a Gelfand triple\n whose inclusion  is compact.\nEquip each trajectory  and its dual time derivative \nwith a Lions–Magenes continuous pivot evolution.\nSupposeon a finite interval  with . Then the family  has compact\nclosure in .\n\nThe compact inclusion produces canonical finite-dimensional spectral trial\nspaces. Their pivot projections converge uniformly on the compact image of\nthe energy unit ball, hence their Bochner lifts make the spatial tails\nuniformly small on every -bounded family.At one fixed trial dimension, the Lions–Magenes pairing identity expresses\nthe projected trajectory increment as the interval integral of the\nmass-inverted restricted dual derivative. The derivative bound gives a\ncommon one-half Hölder modulus, while the quantitative time-Sobolev estimate\ngives a common pivot bound. Finite-dimensional Arzelà–Ascoli therefore makes\nthe projected family precompact. A finite-net argument combines that compact\nprojected family with the uniform spatial tail and yields compact closure in\n.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Weak Evolution\u0009Weak Evolution and Galerkin Approximation\u0009Aubin–Lions Compactness","header":"6.3.1.10.1. Compactness from space and time regularity","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Weak-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Aubin___Lions-Compactness--Compactness-from-space-and-time-regularity"}});